{#
  The form under a post that is still taking comments.

  `commentForm` is on the context only when the post is open — the site switch,
  the closing window and the post's own `comments:` front matter all agree
  before it gets here — so a layout asks `{% if commentForm %}` and nothing
  else. A site replaces this whole section with a
  `partials/comment-form.njk` of its own in the theme it wears; the shape it is
  handed is documented in the theme README under "The comment form".

  `comment-respond` and `comment-reply-title` are the source design's names for
  a form under a post and its heading (decision-16), and the stylesheet styles
  every label, field and button through them — so the contact form wears the
  same class rather than repeating the rules.

  It draws two forms. For a stranger: the name, email and website boxes, the
  honeypot and the loaded-at stamp. For somebody signed in to this site's
  admin — `commentForm.signedInAs` — none of those, because the site already
  knows all three and a session is better evidence than a honeypot, and a
  hidden CSRF token instead, because this form posts under somebody's name.
  A theme that replaces this partial has to draw both.

  It works with JavaScript switched off, and there is no script on it.
#}
<section class="comment-respond" id="respond">
  <h2 class="comment-reply-title">Leave a comment</h2>

  {% if commentNotice %}
  <p class="comment-notice" role="status">{{ commentNotice }}</p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if commentForm.error %}
  <p class="comment-error" role="alert">{{ commentForm.error }}</p>
  {% endif %}

  <form method="post" action="{{ commentForm.action }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="{{ commentForm.fields.post }}" value="{{ commentForm.post }}" />
    {#
      What this comment answers. `commentReplyTo` is the `?reply_to=` on the
      URL, which a Reply link put there and the CMS has already checked names
      an approved comment on this very post; otherwise it is whatever a refused
      submission carried.
    #}
    {% set replyTo = commentReplyTo if commentReplyTo else commentForm.values.inReplyTo %}
    <input type="hidden" name="{{ commentForm.fields.inReplyTo }}" value="{{ replyTo }}" />

    {% if commentReplyingTo %}
    <p class="comment-replying-to">
      Replying to {{ commentReplyingTo }}.
      <a href="{{ page.url }}#respond">Comment on the post instead</a>
    </p>
    {% endif %}

    {% if commentForm.signedInAs %}
    {#
      Signed in to this site. The comment is attributed from the account, so
      there is nothing to type and nothing to get wrong; the token is what
      stops a page on another site posting under this name without them
      knowing.
    #}
    <input type="hidden" name="{{ commentForm.fields.csrf }}" value="{{ commentForm.csrfToken }}" />

    <p class="comment-signed-in">
      Commenting as <a href="{{ commentForm.signedInAs.url }}">{{ commentForm.signedInAs.name }}</a>.
    </p>
    {% else %}
    {#
      When this form was drawn, which is what the minimum submit time is
      measured against. A signed-in form carries none: a session says more
      about whether this is a person than a stopwatch does.
    #}
    <input type="hidden" name="{{ commentForm.fields.loaded }}" value="{{ commentForm.loaded }}" />

    {#
      The honeypot. Hidden from sight and from assistive technology, out of the
      tab order, and told not to autofill: a person never sees it and never
      fills it in, so anything that does is not a person.
    #}
    <div class="comment-trap" aria-hidden="true">
      <label for="comment-website">Website</label>
      <input id="comment-website" name="{{ commentForm.fields.trap }}" type="text" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" value="" />
    </div>

    <p class="comment-field">
      <label for="comment-name">Name</label>
      <input id="comment-name" name="{{ commentForm.fields.name }}" type="text" value="{{ commentForm.values.name }}" maxlength="{{ commentForm.nameLength }}" autocomplete="name" required />
      {% if commentForm.problems.name %}
      <span class="comment-field-error">{{ commentForm.problems.name }}</span>
      {% endif %}
    </p>

    <p class="comment-field">
      <label for="comment-email">Email <span class="comment-optional">(optional, never shown)</span></label>
      <input id="comment-email" name="{{ commentForm.fields.email }}" type="email" value="{{ commentForm.values.email }}" autocomplete="email" />
      {% if commentForm.problems.email %}
      <span class="comment-field-error">{{ commentForm.problems.email }}</span>
      {% endif %}
    </p>

    <p class="comment-field">
      <label for="comment-url">Your site <span class="comment-optional">(optional)</span></label>
      <input id="comment-url" name="{{ commentForm.fields.url }}" type="text" inputmode="url" value="{{ commentForm.values.url }}" autocomplete="url" />
      {% if commentForm.problems.url %}
      <span class="comment-field-error">{{ commentForm.problems.url }}</span>
      {% endif %}
    </p>
    {% endif %}

    <p class="comment-field">
      <label for="comment-body">Comment</label>
      <textarea id="comment-body" name="{{ commentForm.fields.body }}" rows="6" maxlength="{{ commentForm.bodyLength }}" required>{{ commentForm.values.body }}</textarea>
      {% if commentForm.problems.body %}
      <span class="comment-field-error">{{ commentForm.problems.body }}</span>
      {% endif %}
      <span class="comment-hint">Markdown works. Links are marked <code>nofollow ugc</code>; HTML is not.</span>
    </p>

    {#
      "Tell me about replies". Only on a site that can actually send mail —
      `notifiable` is false otherwise — because a box that promised a message
      nothing would ever send would be a lie on a form. The address is the one
      typed above — or the account's, for somebody signed in — is never shown
      anywhere, and every message it sends carries a one-click unsubscribe
      link.
    #}
    {% if commentForm.notifiable %}
    <p class="comment-check">
      <input id="comment-notify" name="{{ commentForm.fields.notify }}" type="checkbox" value="1"{% if commentForm.values.notify %} checked{% endif %} />
      <label for="comment-notify">Email me when somebody replies to this</label>
    </p>
    {% endif %}

    <p class="form-submit">
      <button type="submit">Post comment</button>
    </p>
  </form>
</section>
